A||N||8||V6pack||Engine out from above?||||10||
Z||000000||V6pack||12-25-1999||01:38 PM||||<BR>I'm new here and was told by my friend FD that it was the best. He was right again.  My question, an old one, I have been told it is not difficult to pull a V6 out from the top..no cradle drop.  True, false, opinions? Thanks, Dave M.||||reg||10
Z||000001||dogtired||12-26-1999||12:36 AM||||Your friend FD is right, this is the best Forum!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>But the motor thing, that is a matter of prefrence and skill. I personally have never pulled a fiero motor out the top (various V8's & straight 6's) but with the fiero, there isn't any need for a engine hoist, just a 4X4 and a couple floor jacks and you can do your thing.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> either way is a lot of work, kind of have to look at it and see what suits you better. GOOD LUCK||||reg||6
Z||000002||Linx||12-26-1999||12:57 AM||||The haynes manual say the bottom.<P>But Im gonna stick my neck out here and say, thru the top if you got the manifolds, and heads off.  Why not? Youre probably gonna have 'em off eventually.  Otherwise...the bottom.  Sure is snug in there.<P>Ive approached the engine from both directions and I think it'll come out thru the top quite easily if youve already taken the topside off of it.<p>[This message has been edited by Linx (edited 12-26-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||V6pack||12-26-1999||01:38 AM||||Dogtired and Linx: thanks for the info. I tried for an answer on tech-net. I won't say any more...1 guy said he has it down to 1&1/2 hours, in and out. He says to pull everything off the top of the eng. still in the car and squeeze it up and out. Going to try it soon. I'll tape the cuss words. Glad to be here!||||reg||7
Z||000004||batboy||12-26-1999||08:48 AM||||I've heard that the 4 cylinder engine is easier to remove from the top than the V6, but if you pulled the manifolds and heads off like Linx says, you should be ok. Shaun's website has a description about pulling the engine from the top. I'm not really sure you're gaining much other than not having to drop the tranny so you can still move the car if need be. This is a plus for me with shop space at a premium.<P>Dropping the whole cradle with engine and tranny has lots of advantages too. Once it's all out, it's easy to work on everything. Seals are a snap to replace in the tranny. You can degrease the cradle and tranny plus detail the engine compartment much easier. Aligning the engine and tranny is easier when you are reassembling it together.||||reg||1
Z||000005||V6pack||12-26-1999||11:31 AM||||Thanks for that, batboy.  Space is an issue and I like to roll them around and take my time. The car is fairly oil drip and dirt free underneath.  The trans has been gone through recently too. The engine is okay, I just want a little more push and have decided the 3.4 mod is just right for me...$/horsepower. Maybe I'll do the one after this by the book and see which feels better. Thanks again! ||||reg||1
Z||000006||fieroshop||12-26-1999||12:40 PM||||Actually I have tried both methods and the best is from the bottom. Reason for that is when you take out from the boot you take the wiring harness out with it and you don't have to be a snake to get in and out. By removing from the bottom here is what I do.<BR>1. disconnect the wiring harness from the ecm<BR>2. disconnect everything from the top.(ie hoses,cables, dogbone, also undo the wiring harness by the battery and last undo the stut at the top. The three 23mm nuts at the strut towers. (Don't forget to drain all fluids first)<BR>Next go underneath and disconnect the brake calipers, brake cables and cooling. You are now ready to drop the engine.<BR>George||||reg||7
Z||000007||Sage||12-26-1999||01:13 PM||||If anyone has ever looked at a british shop manual(at least for the MG), they have a tendency to oversimplify the procedures.  Example:When replacing a clutch on some models it is much easier to remove the engine to get to the clutch. Instead of giving you detailed instructions for the engine removal, the manual simply states, remove power plant!  So I told one of my buddies, that the english version of Fiero engine removal would be something like; Step 1)drain fluids:2)disconnect power plant:3)remove vehicle. Just thought someone might get a chuckle from this!?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000008||V6pack||12-26-1999||01:28 PM||||Yes Sage, I can fully appreciate your thoughts on Brit manuals. I'm still looking for my spanner! Then of course there is your boot and bonnet. Seriously, as it relates to a Fiero... a rear suspension drop on a Jag is very similar in weight and difficulty because of the darn in-board brakes/rearend and axle are super heavy and the rear has to be way up in the air.  I'm leaning towards 'by the book' now. They had a good reason for writing it that way I guess. Thanks, Dave  ||||reg||7
